## Visitor Pattern with Java Records and Sealed Types

Modern Java (16+) gives us **records** and **sealed interfaces**, which pair beautifully with the Visitor pattern. Sealed interfaces let the compiler *know* the complete set of subtypes — which makes exhaustive visitors possible and safe.

```java
public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rectangle, Triangle {}

public record Circle(double radius) implements Shape {
    @Override
    public double accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) {
        return visitor.visit(this);
    }
}

public record Rectangle(double width, double height) implements Shape {
    @Override
    public double accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) {
        return visitor.visit(this);
    }
}

public record Triangle(double base, double height) implements Shape {
    @Override
    public double accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) {
        return visitor.visit(this);
    }
}
```

Because the interface is `sealed`, adding a new shape is an *explicit, compiler-checked decision*. If you add a new permitted subtype, every visitor that uses a pattern-matching-style approach will fail to compile until you handle it — a huge safety win over the classic "add a method and hope you found all the subclasses" approach.

Java 21's **pattern matching for switch** offers a related alternative to visitors for such sealed hierarchies:

```java
double area(Shape shape) {
    return switch (shape) {
        case Circle c      -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
        case Rectangle r   -> r.width() * r.height();
        case Triangle t    -> 0.5 * t.base() * t.height();
    }; // exhaustive — no default needed with sealed types
}
```

This "internal visitor" style is more concise for simple cases, but the classic Visitor pattern still wins when you need **runtime-pluggable operations** or want to keep operation classes fully separate with their own state.

