# Visitor Design Pattern in Java: A Complete Guide

## Introduction

The **Visitor Design Pattern** is one of the most misunderstood yet powerful behavioral design patterns in Java. It allows you to add new operations to existing object structures without modifying those structures. This pattern is particularly useful when you have a complex object structure (like a tree or a collection of related objects) and you need to perform various unrelated operations on these objects.

In this comprehensive guide, we'll explore the Visitor pattern in depth, understand when and how to use it, examine real-world examples, and discuss its advantages and potential pitfalls. Whether you're preparing for a Java interview or designing a complex system, understanding this pattern will significantly enhance your software design skills.

![Visitor Design Pattern Overview](https://refactoring.guru/images/patterns/diagrams/visitor/structure.png)

*Image source: [Refactoring Guru - Visitor Pattern](https://refactoring.guru/design-patterns/visitor)*

---

## What is the Visitor Design Pattern?

The Visitor pattern is a **behavioral design pattern** that lets you separate algorithms from the objects on which they operate. It enables you to define a new operation without changing the classes of the elements on which it operates.

### Core Concept

Imagine you have a collection of different types of shapes (Circle, Square, Triangle), and you want to perform various operations on them like calculating area, exporting to XML, or rendering to screen. Without the Visitor pattern, you'd have to add these methods to each shape class, which violates the Open/Closed Principle.

The Visitor pattern solves this by:
1. Creating a **Visitor interface** that declares visit methods for each type of element
2. Having each element class **accept** a visitor
3. The visitor **dispatches** the appropriate method based on the element's type

### The Gang of Four Definition

According to the original Gang of Four (GoF) book *Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software*:

> "Represent an operation to be performed on the elements of an object structure. Visitor lets you define a new operation without changing the classes of the elements on which it operates."

---

## Key Components of the Visitor Pattern

The Visitor pattern consists of five main components:

### 1. Visitor Interface
Declares a set of `visit()` methods, one for each type of concrete element class in the object structure.

### 2. Concrete Visitor
Implements the Visitor interface and defines the actual operations to be performed on each element type.

### 3. Element Interface
Declares an `accept()` method that takes a visitor as an argument.

### 4. Concrete Element
Implements the `accept()` method, typically by calling `visitor.visit(this)`.

### 5. Object Structure
A collection or composite of elements that can be iterated over and visited.

![Visitor Pattern Class Diagram](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/W3sDesign_Visitor_Design_Pattern_UML.jpg/800px-W3sDesign_Visitor_Design_Pattern_UML.jpg)

*Image source: [Wikipedia - Visitor Pattern](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visitor_pattern)*

---

## Basic Implementation in Java

Let's start with a simple example to understand the mechanics of the Visitor pattern.

### Step 1: Define the Element Interface

```java
public interface Shape {
    void accept(ShapeVisitor visitor);
}
```

### Step 2: Create Concrete Elements

```java
public class Circle implements Shape {
    private double radius;

    public Circle(double radius) {
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    public double getRadius() {
        return radius;
    }

    @Override
    public void accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) {
        visitor.visit(this);
    }
}

public class Square implements Shape {
    private double side;

    public Square(double side) {
        this.side = side;
    }

    public double getSide() {
        return side;
    }

    @Override
    public void accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) {
        visitor.visit(this);
    }
}

public class Rectangle implements Shape {
    private double width;
    private double height;

    public Rectangle(double width, double height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    public double getWidth() { return width; }
    public double getHeight() { return height; }

    @Override
    public void accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) {
        visitor.visit(this);
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Define the Visitor Interface

```java
public interface ShapeVisitor {
    void visit(Circle circle);
    void visit(Square square);
    void visit(Rectangle rectangle);
}
```

### Step 4: Implement Concrete Visitors

```java
public class AreaCalculator implements ShapeVisitor {
    private double totalArea = 0;

    @Override
    public void visit(Circle circle) {
        totalArea += Math.PI * circle.getRadius() * circle.getRadius();
    }

    @Override
    public void visit(Square square) {
        totalArea += square.getSide() * square.getSide();
    }

    @Override
    public void visit(Rectangle rectangle) {
        totalArea += rectangle.getWidth() * rectangle.getHeight();
    }

    public double getTotalArea() {
        return totalArea;
    }
}

public class PerimeterCalculator implements ShapeVisitor {
    private double totalPerimeter = 0;

    @Override
    public void visit(Circle circle) {
        totalPerimeter += 2 * Math.PI * circle.getRadius();
    }

    @Override
    public void visit(Square square) {
        totalPerimeter += 4 * square.getSide();
    }

    @Override
    public void visit(Rectangle rectangle) {
        totalPerimeter += 2 * (rectangle.getWidth() + rectangle.getHeight());
    }

    public double getTotalPerimeter() {
        return totalPerimeter;
    }
}
```

### Step 5: Use the Pattern

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Shape> shapes = List.of(
            new Circle(5),
            new Square(4),
            new Rectangle(3, 6)
        );

        AreaCalculator areaCalculator = new AreaCalculator();
        PerimeterCalculator perimeterCalculator = new PerimeterCalculator();

        for (Shape shape : shapes) {
            shape.accept(areaCalculator);
            shape.accept(perimeterCalculator);
        }

        System.out.println("Total Area: " + areaCalculator.getTotalArea());
        System.out.println("Total Perimeter: " + perimeterCalculator.getTotalPerimeter());
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
Total Area: 130.53981633974483
Total Perimeter: 71.41592653589793
```

---

## How Double Dispatch Works

The Visitor pattern relies on a mechanism called **double dispatch**. This is a crucial concept to understand.

In a normal method call (single dispatch), the method that gets executed depends on:
- The runtime type of the object
- The method signature (compile-time)

With **double dispatch**, the executed method depends on:
- The runtime type of the **visitor**
- The runtime type of the **element**

### Why is This Important?

Java doesn't support double dispatch natively. Method overloading is resolved at compile-time based on declared types, not runtime types. The Visitor pattern simulates double dispatch by:

1. First dispatch: `shape.accept(visitor)` — dispatches based on the shape's runtime type
2. Second dispatch: `visitor.visit(this)` — dispatches based on the visitor's runtime type

```java
// Without Visitor pattern - this WON'T work as expected
Shape shape = new Circle(5);
visitor.visit(shape); // Calls visit(Shape), not visit(Circle)!

// With Visitor pattern - correct method is called
shape.accept(visitor); // Correctly calls visit(Circle)
```

This is why the `accept()` method is essential — it captures the actual type of the element at runtime and passes it to the appropriate overloaded `visit()` method.

---

## Real-World Example: Document Processing

Let's look at a more practical example — a document processing system that handles different types of documents.

```java
// Element interface
public interface Document {
    void accept(DocumentVisitor visitor);
}

// Concrete Elements
public class PDFDocument implements Document {
    private String fileName;
    private int pages;

    public PDFDocument(String fileName, int pages) {
        this.fileName = fileName;
        this.pages = pages;
    }

    public String getFileName() { return fileName; }
    public int getPages() { return pages; }

    @Override
    public void accept(DocumentVisitor visitor) {
        visitor.visit(this);
    }
}

public class WordDocument implements Document {
    private String fileName;
    private int wordCount;

    public WordDocument(String fileName, int wordCount) {
        this.fileName = fileName;
        this.wordCount = wordCount;
    }

    public String getFileName() { return fileName; }
    public int getWordCount() { return wordCount; }

    @Override
    public void accept(DocumentVisitor visitor) {
        visitor.visit(this);
    }
}

public class SpreadsheetDocument implements Document {
    private String fileName;
    private int rowCount;

    public SpreadsheetDocument(String fileName, int rowCount) {
        this.fileName = fileName;
        this.rowCount = rowCount;
    }

    public String getFileName() { return fileName; }
    public int getRowCount() { return rowCount; }

    @Override
    public void accept(DocumentVisitor visitor) {
        visitor.visit(this);
    }
}

// Visitor interface
public interface DocumentVisitor {
    void visit(PDFDocument pdf);
    void visit(WordDocument word);
    void visit(SpreadsheetDocument spreadsheet);
}

// Concrete Visitor: Text Extraction
public class TextExtractor implements DocumentVisitor {
    private StringBuilder extractedText = new StringBuilder();

    @Override
    public void visit(PDFDocument pdf) {
        extractedText.append("Extracting text from PDF: ")
                     .append(pdf.getFileName())
                     .append(" (").append(pdf.getPages()).append(" pages)\n");
    }

    @Override
    public void visit(WordDocument word) {
        extractedText.append("Extracting text from Word: ")
                     .append(word.getFileName())
                     .append(" (").append(word.getWordCount()).append(" words)\n");
    }

    @Override
    public void visit(SpreadsheetDocument spreadsheet) {
        extractedText.append("Extracting text from Spreadsheet: ")
                     .append(spreadsheet.getFileName())
                     .append(" (").append(spreadsheet.getRowCount()).append(" rows)\n");
    }

    public String getExtractedText() {
        return extractedText.toString();
    }
}

// Concrete Visitor: Document Statistics
public class DocumentStatistics implements DocumentVisitor {
    private int totalPages = 0;
    private int totalWords = 0;
    private int totalRows = 0;

    @Override
    public void visit(PDFDocument pdf) {
        totalPages += pdf.getPages();
    }

    @Override
    public void visit(WordDocument word) {
        totalWords += word.getWordCount();
    }

    @Override
    public void visit(SpreadsheetDocument spreadsheet) {
        totalRows += spreadsheet.getRowCount();
    }

    public void printStatistics() {
        System.out.println("=== Document Statistics ===");
        System.out.println("Total PDF pages: " + totalPages);
        System.out.println("Total Word count: " + totalWords);
        System.out.println("Total Spreadsheet rows: " + totalRows);
    }
}

// Usage
public class DocumentProcessor {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Document> documents = List.of(
            new PDFDocument("report.pdf", 25),
            new WordDocument("contract.docx", 1500),
            new SpreadsheetDocument("data.xlsx", 500),
            new PDFDocument("manual.pdf", 100)
        );

        TextExtractor extractor = new TextExtractor();
        DocumentStatistics statistics = new DocumentStatistics();

        for (Document doc : documents) {
            doc.accept(extractor);
            doc.accept(statistics);
        }

        System.out.println(extractor.getExtractedText());
        statistics.printStatistics();
    }
}
```

This example demonstrates the true power of the Visitor pattern — we can add new operations (like `CompressDocuments`, `EncryptDocuments`, or `BackupDocuments`) without modifying any of the document classes.

