## 5. End-to-End Implementation: E-Commerce Calculation Engine

Let's build a clean, production-grade implementation of our E-Commerce Cart Engine in Java.

### Using Generics for Return Values (`Visitor<R>`)

In many beginner tutorials, `visit()` and `accept()` return `void`, forcing the visitor to store results in a mutable instance field. While stateful visitors are sometimes useful, defining a **generic return type `<R>`** on the Visitor interface is much cleaner, thread-safe, and functional. Let's use this best practice.

### Step 1: Define the Element Interface (`CartItem.java`)

```java
package com.example.ecommerce.element;

import com.example.ecommerce.visitor.CartItemVisitor;

/**
 * The Element interface. Any product in the shopping cart must implement this.
 */
public interface CartItem {
    <R> R accept(CartItemVisitor<R> visitor);
}
```

### Step 2: Create Concrete Elements

Here are three distinct product types, each with its own domain-specific properties and getters:

```java
package com.example.ecommerce.element;

import com.example.ecommerce.visitor.CartItemVisitor;

public class Book implements CartItem {
    private final String isbn;
    private final String title;
    private final double price;
    private final double weightKg;

    public Book(String isbn, String title, double price, double weightKg) {
        this.isbn = isbn;
        this.title = title;
        this.price = price;
        this.weightKg = weightKg;
    }

    public String getIsbn()     { return isbn; }
    public String getTitle()    { return title; }
    public double getPrice()    { return price; }
    public double getWeightKg() { return weightKg; }

    @Override
    public <R> R accept(CartItemVisitor<R> visitor) {
        // Double dispatch: 'this' is statically known as Book
        return visitor.visit(this);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.example.ecommerce.element;

import com.example.ecommerce.visitor.CartItemVisitor;

public class Electronics implements CartItem {
    private final String brandAndModel;
    private final double price;
    private final double weightKg;
    private final boolean containsLithiumBattery;

    public Electronics(String brandAndModel, double price, double weightKg, boolean containsLithiumBattery) {
        this.brandAndModel = brandAndModel;
        this.price = price;
        this.weightKg = weightKg;
        this.containsLithiumBattery = containsLithiumBattery;
    }

    public String getBrandAndModel()          { return brandAndModel; }
    public double getPrice()                  { return price; }
    public double getWeightKg()               { return weightKg; }
    public boolean isContainsLithiumBattery() { return containsLithiumBattery; }

    @Override
    public <R> R accept(CartItemVisitor<R> visitor) {
        return visitor.visit(this);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.example.ecommerce.element;

import com.example.ecommerce.visitor.CartItemVisitor;

public class PerishableFood implements CartItem {
    private final String name;
    private final double price;
    private final double weightKg;
    private final boolean requiresDeepFreeze;

    public PerishableFood(String name, double price, double weightKg, boolean requiresDeepFreeze) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
        this.weightKg = weightKg;
        this.requiresDeepFreeze = requiresDeepFreeze;
    }

    public String getName()                { return name; }
    public double getPrice()               { return price; }
    public double getWeightKg()            { return weightKg; }
    public boolean isRequiresDeepFreeze()  { return requiresDeepFreeze; }

    @Override
    public <R> R accept(CartItemVisitor<R> visitor) {
        return visitor.visit(this);
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Define the Visitor Interface (`CartItemVisitor.java`)

```java
package com.example.ecommerce.visitor;

import com.example.ecommerce.element.Book;
import com.example.ecommerce.element.Electronics;
import com.example.ecommerce.element.PerishableFood;

/**
 * The Visitor interface parameterized by return type <R>.
 * Adding a new method here forces all ConcreteVisitors to implement it.
 */
public interface CartItemVisitor<R> {
    R visit(Book book);
    R visit(Electronics electronics);
    R visit(PerishableFood food);
}
```

