## Visitor with Records and Sealed Types

Modern Java (16+) gives us **records** and **sealed interfaces**, which pair beautifully with Visitor. Sealing the hierarchy lets the compiler know the complete set of subtypes:

```java
public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rectangle, Triangle {}

public record Circle(double radius) implements Shape {
    @Override
    public double accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) { return visitor.visit(this); }
}

public record Rectangle(double width, double height) implements Shape {
    @Override
    public double accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) { return visitor.visit(this); }
}

public record Triangle(double base, double height) implements Shape {
    @Override
    public double accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) { return visitor.visit(this); }
}
```

Now adding a new permitted subtype is an explicit, compiler-checked decision. Java 21's **pattern matching for switch** also offers a related "internal visitor" alternative for sealed hierarchies:

```java
double area(Shape shape) {
    return switch (shape) {
        case Circle c    -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
        case Rectangle r -> r.width() * r.height();
        case Triangle t  -> 0.5 * t.base() * t.height();
    }; // exhaustive — no default needed
}
```

Pattern-matching switches are more concise for simple cases, but the classic Visitor still wins when you need **runtime-pluggable operations** or visitors that carry their own state.

