## 4. Production-Ready Implementation: E-Commerce Calculation Engine

Let's build a clean, production-ready system where an e-commerce platform needs to calculate shipping charges, generate invoices, and handle customs rules for diverse products.

We will use Java generics to ensure that our visitors can cleanly return values (such as `Double`, `String`, or complex domain receipts) without resorting to mutable internal state or type-casting return values.

### Step 1: The Generic Visitor Interface

```java
package com.example.visitor;

import com.example.visitor.model.Book;
import com.example.visitor.model.Electronics;
import com.example.visitor.model.PerishableFood;

/**
 * Generic Visitor interface.
 * @param <R> The return type of the visit operations.
 */
public interface ItemVisitor<R> {
    R visit(Book book);
    R visit(Electronics electronics);
    R visit(PerishableFood perishableFood);
}
```

### Step 2: The Element Interface

```java
package com.example.visitor;

/**
 * Element interface defining the double dispatch hook.
 */
public interface CartItem {
    <R> R accept(ItemVisitor<R> visitor);
}
```

### Step 3: Concrete Domain Elements

Each domain model manages only its own intrinsic properties:

```java
package com.example.visitor.model;

import com.example.visitor.CartItem;
import com.example.visitor.ItemVisitor;

public class Book implements CartItem {
    private final String isbn;
    private final String title;
    private final double price;
    private final double weightKg;

    public Book(String isbn, String title, double price, double weightKg) {
        this.isbn = isbn;
        this.title = title;
        this.price = price;
        this.weightKg = weightKg;
    }

    public String getIsbn() { return isbn; }
    public String getTitle() { return title; }
    public double getPrice() { return price; }
    public double getWeightKg() { return weightKg; }

    @Override
    public <R> R accept(ItemVisitor<R> visitor) {
        // Dispatch 2: The static type of 'this' is Book.
        // Directs execution to ItemVisitor.visit(Book).
        return visitor.visit(this);
    }
}
```

```java
