## Real-World Example: A Shopping Cart

A more business-like scenario: a cart holds `Book`, `Electronics`, and `DigitalProduct` items, and we want to compute shipping per item type.

```java
public interface CartItem {
    double accept(CartVisitor visitor);
}

public class Book implements CartItem {
    private final double price, weightKg;
    public Book(double price, double weightKg) {
        this.price = price; this.weightKg = weightKg;
    }
    public double getPrice()    { return price; }
    public double getWeightKg() { return weightKg; }

    @Override
    public double accept(CartVisitor visitor) { return visitor.visit(this); }
}

public class Electronics implements CartItem {
    private final double price, weightKg;
    private final boolean fragile;
    public Electronics(double price, double weightKg, boolean fragile) {
        this.price = price; this.weightKg = weightKg; this.fragile = fragile;
    }
    public double getPrice()    { return price; }
    public double getWeightKg() { return weightKg; }
    public boolean isFragile()  { return fragile; }

    @Override
    public double accept(CartVisitor visitor) { return visitor.visit(this); }
}

public class DigitalProduct implements CartItem {
    private final double price;
    public DigitalProduct(double price) { this.price = price; }
    public double getPrice() { return price; }

    @Override
    public double accept(CartVisitor visitor) { return visitor.visit(this); }
}
```

```java
public interface CartVisitor {
    double visit(Book book);
    double visit(Electronics electronics);
    double visit(DigitalProduct digitalProduct);
}

public class ShippingCostVisitor implements CartVisitor {
    private static final double RATE_PER_KG = 2.50;
    private static final double FRAGILE_SURCHARGE = 10.00;

    @Override
    public double visit(Book book) {
        return book.getWeightKg() * RATE_PER_KG;
    }

    @Override
    public double visit(Electronics e) {
        double cost = e.getWeightKg() * RATE_PER_KG;
        return e.isFragile() ? cost + FRAGILE_SURCHARGE : cost;
    }

    @Override
    public double visit(DigitalProduct d) {
        return 0.0; // digital goods ship instantly
    }
}
```

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class ShoppingCart {
    private final List<CartItem> items = new ArrayList<>();

    public void add(CartItem item) { items.add(item); }

    public double calculateShipping(CartVisitor visitor) {
        return items.stream()
                    .mapToDouble(item -> item.accept(visitor))
                    .sum();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ShoppingCart cart = new ShoppingCart();
        cart.add(new Book(29.99, 0.8));
        cart.add(new Electronics(999.00, 3.2, true));
        cart.add(new DigitalProduct(19.99));

        System.out.printf("Shipping: $%.2f%n",
                cart.calculateShipping(new ShippingCostVisitor()));
        // (0.8 * 2.5) + (3.2 * 2.5 + 10) + 0 = 22.00
    }
}
```

Tomorrow, if marketing wants a **loyalty discount visitor** or a **tax visitor**, you write one new class. The item classes never change.

