## Introduction

The **Visitor design pattern** is one of the most powerful — and most misunderstood — behavioral design patterns in Java. It lets you add new operations to a family of classes **without modifying those classes**. Instead of the objects carrying the behavior themselves, they simply "accept" a visitor, and the visitor does the work.

If you've ever faced a class hierarchy where every new requirement (export to XML, compute statistics, validate, render) forced you to add methods to every single class, the Visitor pattern was designed for you.

In this article we'll walk through the pattern in plain English, explain the clever mechanism that makes it work (*double dispatch*), and build complete, runnable Java examples — including modern Java with records and sealed interfaces.

---

## What Is the Visitor Design Pattern?

The Visitor pattern is one of the **23 classic design patterns** described in *Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software* by Erich Gamma, Richard Helm, Ralph Johnson, and John Vlissides — the famous **"Gang of Four" (GoF)**. It belongs to the **behavioral** category.

The GoF definition:

> *"Represent an operation to be performed on the elements of an object structure. Visitor lets you define a new operation without changing the classes of the elements on which it operates."*

### An analogy

Think of a tax auditor visiting a company. The auditor doesn't permanently change how any department works. Each department *receives* the auditor ("accepts" them) and hands over its records so the auditor can do the audit. If tomorrow you need a *health inspector* instead, you simply send a different visitor — the departments themselves don't change at all.

---

## The Problem It Solves

Suppose you're building a document application with elements like `Heading`, `Paragraph`, and `Image`. Requirements roll in:

- Export the document to HTML.
- Export it to plain text.
- Count total words.
- Spell-check everything.
- Generate a table of contents.

### The naive approach

Add a method per operation to every class:

```java
class Heading {
    public String toHtml() { /* ... */ }
    public String toPlainText() { /* ... */ }
    public int countWords() { /* ... */ }
    // ...and so on
}

class Paragraph {
    public String toHtml() { /* ... */ }
    public String toPlainText() { /* ... */ }
    public int countWords() { /* ... */ }
}
```

### Why this hurts

1. **Violation of the Open/Closed Principle** — every new operation forces you to open and modify every element class.
2. **Bloated classes** — element classes fill up with unrelated concerns (HTML generation, statistics, spell-checking).
3. **Scattered algorithms** — "export to HTML" logic is smeared across many classes instead of living in one place.

The Visitor pattern flips the trade-off: it keeps **elements stable** and puts each **whole operation into one visitor class**. You get the Open/Closed Principle for *operations*, at the cost of making it harder to add new *element types* — more on that trade-off later.

---

## Structure and Participants

The pattern has five participants:

| Participant | Role |
|---|---|
| **Visitor** (interface) | Declares a `visit()` method for each concrete element type. |
| **ConcreteVisitor** | Implements the operation for each element type; may accumulate state. |
| **Element** (interface) | Declares an `accept(Visitor v)` method. |
| **ConcreteElement** | Implements `accept()`, which calls `visitor.visit(this)`. |
| **ObjectStructure** | A collection or tree of elements that can be traversed. |

![Visitor Pattern UML Diagram](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/W3sDesign_Visitor_Design_Pattern_UML.jpg/800px-W3sDesign_Visitor_Design_Pattern_UML.jpg)

*Image source: [Wikipedia – Visitor pattern](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visitor_pattern)*

The crucial detail: `ConcreteElement.accept(visitor)` calls `visitor.visit(this)` — and because `this` has the precise concrete type, the *correct* overloaded `visit()` method is chosen. That's the heart of the pattern.

---

## The Key Trick: Double Dispatch

Java uses **single dispatch**: which method runs at runtime depends only on the runtime type of the *receiver* (the object before the dot). Method **overloading**, on the other hand, is resolved at **compile time** based on the declared (static) type of the arguments:

```java
Shape shape = new Circle(5);
visitor.visit(shape); // calls visit(Shape), NOT visit(Circle)!
```

Even though `shape` holds a `Circle` at runtime, the compiler picked `visit(Shape)` because the variable's declared type is `Shape`. This is why you can't just call `visitor.visit(element)` from the outside.

The Visitor pattern works around it with **double dispatch** — two virtual calls:

1. **First dispatch:** `element.accept(visitor)` — Java picks the right `accept()` based on the element's *runtime type*.
2. **Second dispatch:** inside `accept()`, the element calls `visitor.visit(this)` — and `this` now has the precise concrete type, so the correct overloaded `visit()` is chosen.

```
Client                     Element (Circle)               Visitor
   |                             |                           |
   |-- circle.accept(visitor) -->|                           |
   |                             |-- visitor.visit(this) --->|
   |                             |   (this is a Circle)      |
   |       The correct visit(Circle) runs — no instanceof!   |
```

The result: the runtime type of the *element* determines which method executes — a simulation of dynamic dispatch on the argument, which Java doesn't provide natively.

