## Modern Java: Records, Sealed Types, and Pattern Matching

Java 16+ **records** and **sealed interfaces** pair beautifully with the Visitor pattern. Sealed interfaces let the compiler know the *complete* set of subtypes:

```java
public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rectangle, Triangle {
    double accept(ShapeVisitor visitor);
}

public record Circle(double radius) implements Shape {
    @Override
    public double accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) {
        return visitor.visit(this);
    }
}

public record Rectangle(double width, double height) implements Shape {
    @Override
    public double accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) {
        return visitor.visit(this);
    }
}

public record Triangle(double base, double height) implements Shape {
    @Override
    public double accept(ShapeVisitor visitor) {
        return visitor.visit(this);
    }
}
```

Java 21's **pattern matching for `switch`** offers a concise alternative for such hierarchies:

```java
double area(Shape shape) {
    return switch (shape) {
        case Circle c    -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
        case Rectangle r -> r.width() * r.height();
        case Triangle t  -> 0.5 * t.base() * t.height();
    }; // exhaustive — no default needed with sealed types
}
```

Both approaches — the classic visitor and the pattern-matching switch — are compiler-checked and exhaustive: if you add a new `Shape` subtype, the code fails to compile until you handle it. The classic Visitor still wins when you need **runtime-pluggable operations** or want each operation as a separate class with its own state; pattern matching wins on conciseness for simple cases. Read more about sealed types in the [Java Language Specification (JEP 409)](https://openjdk.org/jeps/409) and [JEP 441: Pattern Matching for switch](https://openjdk.org/jeps/441).

